{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795700285,"nonce":"oeEdhktKxGd4kGtjSSGioIfMNIk=","title":"AOT: a panic in a second Rust library crashes a Linux program","message":"A desktop `ash --build` on Linux with Rust static libraries as `--native-lib` segfaults when a second library panics. Found by the four-library test added for df535ff, on both Linux CI jobs. The older Rust library test never panicked, so it did not show this.\n\nCause: each Rust object carries a COMDAT group named `DW.ref.rust_eh_personality`, which its unwind table uses to find the personality. llvm/rust_isolate.rs makes the group's signature symbol local with objcopy. GNU objcopy keeps the COMDAT flag, so the final link still matches the groups by name, keeps one library's and discards the others.\n\n- With GNU ld the program links and crashes during the unwind.\n- With lld the link fails: \"relocation refers to a symbol in a discarded section: DW.ref.rust_eh_personality\".\n\nThe runtime is a Rust library too, so one library is probably enough to break a panic inside the runtime. That case is not tested.\n\nAndroid is not affected with the NDK: its llvm-objcopy clears the flag itself when it makes the signature local. Four libraries link with NDK r27. The result was not run.\n\nReproduced by hand on Linux x86_64 with four static libraries, isolated with the commands ash runs, and linked into a C driver.\n\nConsidered: `--force-group-allocation` on the merge fixes GNU ld and recent lld, but the lld in NDK r27 rejects it.\n\nFix: after objcopy, clear the COMDAT flag of every group whose signature symbol is local.","files":null}]}